# Pathfinder

## Lore

Pathfinders are Wilderness Scouts who cut new trails and know one or more regions in remarkable detail. For isolated villages and wilderness folk a Pathfinder is often the only link to the outside: carrying spoken messages, warnings of raiders, monsters, war or plague, and letters, parcels and medicine from place to place. They bring news and stories too, which makes them welcome company for scouts and trappers who have been alone for weeks.

Some keep to one range that may cover hundreds of miles; others roam just to see what lies past the next ridge. They are skilled mapmakers, much sought after by communities that need maps, and they lead settlers, travellers and adventurers through country that looks impassable. Pay is often small, and many Pathfinders take their reward in good company and a hot meal, so they tend to be sociable people and good storytellers or musicians.

That same knowledge makes them targets: raiders, spies and invaders seize them for their routes or kill them to deny them to an enemy. Many Pathfinders of fallen Tolkeen are missing and presumed dead.

## GM Notes

Cross-Country Pacing: the Pathfinder paces exertion, food and water to cover long distances on foot, horseback or mountain bicycle, and with Land Navigation: Regional can predict a trip''s length within 2D6x10 minutes (6D6 hours in unfamiliar country; a party in tow takes twice as long). On a bicycle he reaches three times his running speed on level ground and twice running speed cross-country, for P.E. x10 minutes; on foot, full running speed cross-country for P.E. x5 minutes. A scout never reveals his best routes unless it is an emergency.

Trail Blazing (20% +5% before the class bonus): cuts and marks trails for others to follow.

Cartography: a successful roll gives an accurate, attractive map; a failure gets details wrong or missing and places off by 1D10 miles. Computer mapping needs Computer Operation.

Roadwise applies only inside the region of his Land Navigation: Regional.

Starting kit beyond what the sheet lists: map making and survey equipment in a hard case, six pieces of white chalk, a mechanical pen with 24 refills, 1D6 pens or markers, two pocket note pads, 6D6 loose sheets of paper, a pocket calculator, a laser range-finder, 1D4 waterproof sacks, a folding utility knife and a dozen chemical lights. A character with a Computer skill has a portable or pocket computer.

Weapons: one for each W.P.; Pathfinders favour light weapons such as Wilk''s lasers over heavy ones.
', 'published', 'data-script'
WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'pathfinder');


-- Read the result back rather than trusting the exit code. d1-apply prints
-- these, and a CR in the stored markdown means the checkout mangled the file.
SELECT class_id, name, status, length(markdown) AS bytes, instr(markdown, char(13)) > 0 AS has_cr
  FROM imported_classes WHERE class_id = 'pathfinder';

-- Records this run. REQUIRED: the smoke test fails a data script that has no
-- footer, or whose footer names a different file.
INSERT INTO data_script_runs (filename) VALUES ('add-pathfinder-class.sql');
